WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.739 --> 00:00:03.627
- I like math.

00:00:03.627 --> 00:00:05.515
I like modeling.

00:00:05.515 --> 00:00:06.932
I like computers.

00:00:08.363 --> 00:00:10.069
But what I want to do is be able to use it

00:00:10.069 --> 00:00:13.205
to help people and so
it's really interesting

00:00:13.205 --> 00:00:16.704
or kind of exciting that
we can use these tools

00:00:16.704 --> 00:00:19.871
in helping in this humanitarian cause.

00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:24.744
- [Narrator] Although the food bank

00:00:24.744 --> 00:00:26.412
of central and eastern North Carolina is

00:00:26.412 --> 00:00:28.839
the largest in the state, they still can't

00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:30.706
reach everyone.

00:00:30.706 --> 00:00:33.340
- I don't think people
realize the magnitude

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.676
of food insecurity.

00:00:35.676 --> 00:00:38.578
We believe there are about 680,000 people

00:00:38.578 --> 00:00:41.330
that are food insecure at some point

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.146
during the month.

00:00:43.146 --> 00:00:47.229
We can't get enough food
in to solve the problem.

00:00:49.162 --> 00:00:52.376
- [Narrator] Enter Dr. Ivy and Dr. Davis.

00:00:52.376 --> 00:00:54.072
They're engineers who realized analytics

00:00:54.072 --> 00:00:56.956
could revolutionize
how food banks operate.

00:00:56.956 --> 00:01:00.070
They reached out to see
how they could help.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.153
- The food bank gave us tons of data,

00:01:03.153 --> 00:01:05.969
and you know initially we
were like data, data, data.

00:01:05.969 --> 00:01:09.766
But data can be like a
blessing and a curse.

00:01:09.766 --> 00:01:11.345
- [Narrator] They mined
through millions of records

00:01:11.345 --> 00:01:14.588
and translated the numbers
into useful information.

00:01:14.588 --> 00:01:17.094
- Some of the models
that we have developed

00:01:17.094 --> 00:01:19.174
try to predict the amount of food

00:01:19.174 --> 00:01:21.007
that might be donated.

00:01:22.364 --> 00:01:25.478
The other type of models
also try to estimate

00:01:25.478 --> 00:01:28.444
how many people might be in need of food.

00:01:28.444 --> 00:01:31.944
We try to pull in data about unemployment,

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:36.762
poverty, to see if we
can get a better estimate

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:40.845
of the true need in the
counties that they serve.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.226
- [Narrator] That information
gives the food bank

00:01:43.226 --> 00:01:44.858
an efficient way to predict changes

00:01:44.858 --> 00:01:47.117
in supply and demand so they can reduce

00:01:47.117 --> 00:01:48.919
food waste while maximizing the number

00:01:48.919 --> 00:01:51.319
of people they can reach.

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:53.047
- What they have done is given us

00:01:53.047 --> 00:01:54.541
why we should do certain things

00:01:54.541 --> 00:01:56.151
and how we should do them, but instead

00:01:56.151 --> 00:01:58.061
of saying, "Well that just feels right,"

00:01:58.061 --> 00:02:01.037
we're able to say, "Well
the data tells us."

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:02.787
It's very cool stuff.

00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:06.573
- We're making an impact.

00:02:06.573 --> 00:02:09.485
And we have the potential
to do so much more.

00:02:09.485 --> 00:02:12.482
The work we do is easily applicable

00:02:12.482 --> 00:02:14.165
to other food banks.

00:02:14.165 --> 00:02:16.546
And I think that we'll
be able to figure out

00:02:16.546 --> 00:02:19.102
how to, you know, reduce
the number of families

00:02:19.102 --> 00:02:20.852
who have hunger need.

